# Local end-to-end runs — `npm run test:e2e`.
#
# Copy to `.env.e2e` and fill in. That file is gitignored and is the only env
# file the suite reads: `specs/mission.md` § Constraints makes every movement a
# test records permanent, so pointing this at the database `.env.local` names
# would leave weights in the shared development branch that nothing can remove.
#
# In CI none of this exists — `.github/workflows/ci.yml` puts the same
# variables in the job's environment directly.

# A throwaway Postgres, never Neon development and never production. The CI job
# runs the same postgres:17 as a service container; locally:
#
#   docker run -d --name feedstock-e2e \
#     -e POSTGRES_USER=postgres -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres \
#     -e POSTGRES_DB=feedstock -p 55432:5432 postgres:17
#
# Both are required and both must point at the throwaway database.
# `prisma.config.ts` migrates over DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED and falls back to
# `.env.local` when it is unset, so omitting it migrates the development branch
# without failing. `src/lib/db.ts` reads DATABASE_URL. There is no pooler here,
# so the two are the same string.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:55432/feedstock"
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED="postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:55432/feedstock"

# The Clerk development instance. `clerkSetup` refuses a production secret key
# outright, so this cannot be pointed at production by accident.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="pk_test_..."
CLERK_SECRET_KEY="sk_test_..."

# Read at build time. Without it `redirectToSignIn` in `src/proxy.ts` sends the
# browser to Clerk's hosted page instead of this app's own, and `auth.spec.ts`
# lands on a host it does not expect.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_URL="/sign-in"

# The account global setup signs in as, provisioned with
# `npm run provision -- <email>`. No password: the sign-in goes over a
# short-lived Backend API ticket minted with CLERK_SECRET_KEY.
E2E_USER_EMAIL="e2e@pulltorefresh.team"
